diagnosis_text:
A full multi-layer diagnostic was performed on the PC0 (20.1.1.10) → PC12 (120.1.1.10) path in response to a reported "clockwork" 60–120 s pulsing/slowdown symptom. Phase 1 reachability showed 0% packet loss in both directions. The active forwarding path was confirmed via traceroute as PC0 → r1 → r11 → r12 → PC12, with r11 acting as the OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution boundary: r11 runs both EIGRP AS 100 (learning 120.0.0.0/8 from r12 via the EE/112.x segment) and OSPF (redistributing those EIGRP routes into area 0.0.0.3 so that r1 and the rest of the backbone can reach PC12). This redistribution has been stable for the entire lab uptime (~14 minutes): the type-5 external LSA for 120.0.0.0/8 carries LS-age=865 s and sequence 80000001, r1's FIB entry has been installed for 11+ minutes, and FRR logs on r1, r11, r12, and r8 contain only normal startup events with zero reconvergence messages after 09:04:17. OSPF and EIGRP adjacencies are fully converged with no active or recently-active flaps. TC inspection shows only default fq_codel on every interface, no traffic-shaping or netem rules. No ACL or firewall rules are present. Interface counters show zero errors and zero drops on all path devices. No cron, at, or systemd timer jobs exist that could periodically modify routing or TC rules. The r8 area-2 misconfiguration (orphaned area with no peers) and the OSPF VLink between r4 and r8 are both stable and non-disruptive to this path. The reported periodic pulsing is unconfirmed: no mechanism, no historical trace, and no current anomaly was found.
=== END ===